  {
    "episode_id": 91892,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91892.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 19,
    "turn_text": "Well, you know, Henry's 11 and Theo's 16. And, you know, children are struggling just to be able to do remote learning effectively. It's hard. And I just love our teachers now more than ever because It's literally a sacrifice to everything. It has so much patience and are so wonderful. We should be paying them double or triple what they get paid. I think every parent in America shares my views at this point. So they're really struggling in their own lives to have to be students and how to be kids. They don't get to go play with friends. They don't get to do their sports. They don't get to do the things that make their life fun and diverse and well-rounded. And I'm worried because it's drawing more kids just into computers and online. And so the only play time they have is online gaming with friends, which at least they're doing with friends, but it's not the same. And so I'm worried about physical fitness. I'm worried about mental health. I'm worried about being able to build resilience. They don't mind being out of the scooter all day long, but I can tell you every parent mind that and the more very stressed out about it. And all those other things kids used to do, go to school, go to lunch, have recess, you know, do sports after school, that's all eliminated. And it's going to harm the well-being and health of our children. Um, the, we are very blessed. Uh, we've not had a loved one in our family who has passed because of COVID-19, but we do have elderly grandparents and be worried about them every day. Um, and so, you know, it's just a stress that there's an increased anxiety that every family in America is facing. And for those who have already lost loved ones, it's devastating and to not be able to be with a parent or a grandparent who's in a disability facility to not be able to see them, to not be able to be with them when they're ill is so heartbreaking. And I have a lot of girlfriends that I talk to a lot about their parents and most of them are in a disability facility and are weathering the storm alone. So it's hard. And I think for kids, we won't really know the impact for a while, but I think it is deeply affecting them.",
